  "fixture_kind": "public_exact-list_card_data",
  "rulings": [
    {
      "comment": "Trophy Mage's ability finds an artifact card with mana value exactly 3.",
      "oracle_id": "08afc0d7-192f-4ab6-b6a0-c4265cf5e225",
      "published_at": "2017-02-09",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a card in a player's library has {X} in its mana cost, X is 0 for the purpose of determining its mana value.",
      "oracle_id": "5104053a-d394-4b00-82a4-60fd9f051a6e",
      "published_at": "2024-11-08",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Effects that instruct you to reveal or look at cards from the top of you library will still work. Only effects that use the word \"search\" are affected.",
      "oracle_id": "5794b58e-f3b6-4637-b288-283b3a72a718",
      "published_at": "2008-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If an effect says \"Search your library . . . then shuffle your library,\" you shuffle you libraries even though you can't search.",
      "oracle_id": "5794b58e-f3b6-4637-b288-283b3a72a718",
      "published_at": "2008-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If an effect says \"You may search your library . . . If you do, shuffle your library\" or \"You may search your library . . . then shuffle your library,\" you can't choose to search, so you won't shuffle.",
      "oracle_id": "5794b58e-f3b6-4637-b288-283b3a72a718",
      "published_at": "2008-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The only difference between a colored artifact and a colorless artifact is, obviously, its color. Unlike most artifacts, a colored artifact requires colored mana to cast. Also unlike most artifacts, a colored artifact has a color in all zones. It will interact with cards that care about color. Other than that, a colored artifact behaves just like any other artifact. It will interact as normal with any card that cares about artifacts, such as Shatter or Arcbound Ravager.",
      "oracle_id": "5794b58e-f3b6-4637-b288-283b3a72a718",
      "published_at": "2008-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a card has {X} in its mana cost, X is 0.",
      "oracle_id": "5859fd59-8732-414a-8932-007980af495d",
      "published_at": "2015-06-22",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Ezuri's first ability can't affect itself, but its second ability does.",
      "oracle_id": "6734497c-16f0-4c4b-ba24-337333511fc6",
      "published_at": "2011-01-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Only Elf creatures you control when the last ability resolves will get +3/+3 and gain trample until end of turn. Non-Elf creatures that become Elves or Elf creatures that enter under your control later in the turn won't get the bonuses.",
      "oracle_id": "6734497c-16f0-4c4b-ba24-337333511fc6",
      "published_at": "2011-01-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a token is exiled this way, it will cease to exist and won't return to the battlefield.",
      "oracle_id": "6879f5ce-7a1b-4606-bad1-885779b0d456",
      "published_at": "2018-03-16",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Once the exiled permanent returns, it's considered a new object with no relation to the object that it was. Auras attached to the exiled permanent will be put into their owners' graveyards. Equipment attached to the exiled permanent will become unattached and remain on the battlefield. Any counters on the exiled permanent will cease to exist.",
      "oracle_id": "6879f5ce-7a1b-4606-bad1-885779b0d456",
      "published_at": "2018-03-16",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The returned card won't be the target of any spells or abilities that targeted it before. Any spells that don't target, such as Akroma's Vengeance, will still affect it.",
      "oracle_id": "6879f5ce-7a1b-4606-bad1-885779b0d456",
      "published_at": "2018-03-16",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "When an effect returns the exiled card \"under your control,\" you control it indefinitely after that. In a multiplayer game, if a player leaves the game, all cards that player owns leave as well. If you leave the game, any creatures you control from Cloudshift's effect are exiled.",
      "oracle_id": "6879f5ce-7a1b-4606-bad1-885779b0d456",
      "published_at": "2018-03-16",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Elvish Archdruid's first ability affects only other Elves you control. However, Elvish Archdruid's second ability counts all Elves you control — including itself.",
      "oracle_id": "6e2c2423-d854-4478-99e6-64f29851f026",
      "published_at": "2009-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Elvish Archdruid's activated ability is a mana ability. It doesn't use the stack and players can't respond to it.",
      "oracle_id": "6e2c2423-d854-4478-99e6-64f29851f026",
      "published_at": "2011-09-22",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The card you find must be both an artifact and a creature.",
      "oracle_id": "77ae7caa-e180-4efb-9348-ab389364b186",
      "published_at": "2009-02-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a card in a player’s library has {X} in its mana cost, X is considered to be 0.",
      "oracle_id": "cada3481-cc2b-4412-b9b5-0436af53aad2",
      "published_at": "2019-06-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Your opponents may cast spells in response to the last ability of Ranger-Captain of Eos. The ability won’t affect those spells and it won’t affect spells that those players cast before you activated it. (In other words, the ability can’t be used to counter a spell.)",
      "oracle_id": "cada3481-cc2b-4412-b9b5-0436af53aad2",
      "published_at": "2019-06-14",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "After the search is complete, the entire library is shuffled.",
      "oracle_id": "d9517c5d-66d0-4178-96fb-a8c04f311ad8",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Each search works exactly the same as normal except that the opponent doing the searching sees only the top four cards of that library. They can't look at the other cards in that library at all. Cards not in the top four cards of the library can't be found in the search, even if they're identifiable in some manner.",
      "oracle_id": "d9517c5d-66d0-4178-96fb-a8c04f311ad8",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "For effects that check whether a player searched a library or may search a library, searching the top four cards of that library counts as searching that library.",
      "oracle_id": "d9517c5d-66d0-4178-96fb-a8c04f311ad8",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If an effect uses the word \"search\" once but allows an opponent to search for multiple cards, the top four cards remain the same throughout the entire search. Finding one card won't cause the fifth card to become included in the search.",
      "oracle_id": "d9517c5d-66d0-4178-96fb-a8c04f311ad8",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If Dryad Arbor is changed into another basic land type (such as by Sea's Claim), it continues to be a creature and a Dryad.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2011-09-22",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Although originally printed with a characteristic-defining ability that defined its color, this card now has a color indicator. This color indicator can't be affected by text-changing effects (such as the one created by Crystal Spray), although color-changing effects can still overwrite it.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2013-06-07",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Dryad Arbor is played as a land. It doesn't use the stack, it's not a spell, it can't be responded to, it has no mana cost, and it counts as your land play for the turn.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Due to its color indicator (appearing to the left of its type line), Dryad Arbor is green. Color indicators apply in all zones, not just the battlefield.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Forest is a land type and Dryad is a creature type.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If Dryad Arbor is changed into another basic land type, it continues to be a green Dryad creature.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a Dryad Arbor gains flash, or you have the ability to play Dryad Arbor as though it had flash (due to Teferi, Mage of Zhalfir or Scout's Warning, for example), you can ignore the normal timing rules for when during your turn you can play a land, but not any other restrictions. You can't play Dryad Arbor during another player's turn, and you can't play Dryad Arbor if you don't have any land plays remaining.",
      "oracle_id": "e996cd67-739c-40f4-b276-0042acf26c71",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    }
  ],
